# Demote the RUNNING A/B image. Triggered ONLY by retroforge-boot-good.service's OnFailure= --
# hence no [Install] -- when the boot could not be confirmed: the session marker vanished under
# the 30s re-check, or the confirmation itself could not be written.
#
# `set-state self bad` puts the booted slot's boot entry at +0 (no tries left), so OpenABL sorts it
# last and the NEXT boot goes to the other slot. It is idempotent, and on a degraded boot where
# the booted slot cannot be identified at all it fails harmlessly rather than guessing a slot.
#
# WHY A WHOLE UNIT FOR ONE COMMAND. systemd has no per-service "run this if the service failed"
# verb. The plausible-looking ExecOnFailure= is not a systemd directive: the parser logs
# "Unknown key name" and carries on, so a service carrying it looks correct and does nothing.
# retroforge shipped exactly that for the whole of phase 5's first pass, which meant the OS half of
# the rollback design was never actually wired up.
#
# ExecStopPost= with a $SERVICE_RESULT test would also work, but it runs inside the failing
# service's own scope -- so a service that failed by being killed drags this with it. A separate
# unit is what OnFailure= is for.
[Unit]
Description=retroforge demote this A/B image after a failed boot confirmation
Documentation=file:///usr/bin/retroforge-bootctl
ConditionPathExists=/run/retroforge/boot

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# NEVER DEMOTE A SLOT WHILE THE SYSTEM IS GOING DOWN. Anything still mid-flight at poweroff gets
# SIGTERMed and can fail for that reason alone, and a shutdown says nothing about slot health --
# demoting there sends the NEXT boot to the other slot, which on a release card is empty.
#
# systemd has no built-in "not shutting down" condition. DefaultDependencies= already gives this
# unit Conflicts=shutdown.target, and on the 2026-08-10 HW occurrence that is what refused the job
# ("Transaction ... is destructive") -- but only because a conflicting stop job happened to be
# queued first. Lose that race and the demote runs. ExecCondition= closes it: a non-zero exit
# SKIPS the unit (systemd v243+) rather than failing it, which is the semantics wanted here.
#
# is-system-running exits non-zero for `degraded` too, so the state is matched on stdout rather
# than on its exit status -- a degraded-but-running system must still be able to demote itself.
ExecCondition=/bin/sh -c '! /usr/bin/systemctl is-system-running | grep -qx stopping'
ExecStart=/usr/bin/retroforge-bootctl set-state self bad
